			<div class="content" id="post-content-1530552">
									

<p>Yeah. This is one of those &quot;delayed oh SHIT&quot; articles. Consider the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>2/3 of the human food supply is made up of rice, maize, and wheat (in that order). The planet's <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carrying_capacity#Food_supply_and_consumption">carrying capacity</a> is stretched as it is. &quot;Massive starvation&quot; doesn't begin to cover it - this would result in major global depopulation. As in, cut human population in half, easily. All the social disorder which would follow from such a massive die-back would result in further casualties, as would the inevitable wars over the remaining viable food supplies.</li>
<li>The majority of domesticated agricultural animals eat grasses, either directly (e.g. grass- or pasture-fed livestock) or indirectly (e.g. livestock fed processed feed, which is often based at least partially from grasses).</li>
<li>A great many wild herbivores and omnivores eat (among other things) grasses. Most wild carnivores and omnivores also eat the aforementioned grass-eaters.</li>
<li>There is a <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dust_bowl">historical instance</a> of what happens when widespread grasses that hold erosion at bay die. Lets just say it ended badly for all involved.</li>
</ul>
<p>So, why would the Foundation work its tail off to contain this? Because having it uncontained would risk XK level ramifications, and the Foundation doesn't let that sort of thing happen if it can help it.</p>
<p>Also, I'm a political scientist, not a biologist, and I had little difficulty following. An important thing to note for those who've not had any biology or chemistry: LD<sub>50</sub> is short for &quot;median lethal dose&quot;, a measurement of the dose of a toxin, radiation, or pathogen required to kill half the members of a tested population after a specified test duration.</p>

